  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1) Thunderstorms are expected Tuesday, with a chance of\nsevere weather.\n\nAs surface high pressure over New Jersey drifts off the Atlantic\ncoast this afternoon, a relatively dry and stable air mass over the \nOhio Valley will slowly begin to modify, with increasing southerly \nflow bringing moisture into the area from the south. There is also \nstill some wildfire smoke in the area, though it is expected that \nthis will move northward and diminish this evening. Relatively quiet \nconditions are expected through the majority of the overnight hours.\n\nA deep upper trough and closed surface low are forecast to move east\nthrough northern Ontario on Tuesday morning, as the axis of the \ntrough (and an associated surface cold front) are forecast to move\nout of the upper Midwest and into the Ohio Valley at some point on\nTuesday. Forcing associated with this feature will bring\nthunderstorms to the ILN forecast area on Tuesday, with some severe\nweather possible. Confidence is very high that thunderstorms will\noccur, but confidence in the overall severe magnitude and timing are\nsomewhat low, as early-day storms will have an impact on the\nmesoscale environment and additional development.\n\nLater today (Monday) it is expected that one or several clusters of\nsevere thunderstorms will develop over the upper Midwest. These\nstorms are expected to move southeast toward Indiana and Ohio during\nthe early morning hours on Tuesday. The storms will probably be in a\nweakening state with the loss of diurnal heating, but there will be \nenough theta-e advection aloft to keep up to 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE \naround (even if much of this is elevated). Overall bulk shear will \nactually be increasing at this time, so despite the limited \ninstability, these storms may bring a marginal risk for severe \nweather (primarily wind). The timing for this first round of storms, \nassuming the storms hold together, will generally be 6AM to 12PM.\n\nAdditional storms are likely to develop heading into Tuesday\nafternoon -- perhaps an intensification of any leftover activity\nfrom the morning, or perhaps new development in areas of significant\ndestabilization or remnant outflow boundaries. It would be very hard\nto say exactly when and where this may occur, as it is all dependent\non mesoscale details from the leftover morning storms. Instability\nwill likely recover to some degree, with SBCAPE values of 1000-2000\nJ/kg on Tuesday afternoon, though the higher values are dependent on\nareas where clearing occurs. Dewpoints getting into the lower to\npossibly middle 70s will also contribute to the destabilization, \nregardless of clearing and insolation. Shear will be notably high for\nJuly, with 500mb WNW flow of 50-60 knots, 0-6km bulk shear of about \n60 knots, and 40-45 knots of 0-3km shear. This shear is easily \nsufficient for organized storms, potentially with some supercellular\nstructures early in storm development, but with a likely trend \ntoward clusters and bowing segments. This suggests a primary threat \nof damaging wind. Some severe hail is possible, particularly with any\nsupercells that develop, though mid level lapse rates and an overall\nwarm environment would limit this somewhat. A tornado or two is also\npossible, but this does not appear to be the main risk, as marginal \n0-1km shear and rather veered surface flow may keep this risk more \nlimited. Finally, precipitable water values of around 2 inches \nsupport very heavy rainfall. Though most of the activity will be \nprogressive (given the generally strong wind flow) some training \nstorms may occur, so there is a threat for some localized flash \nflooding as well.\n\nIn summary, severe weather is certainly possible tomorrow, but the\nafternoon threat remains somewhat conditional on destabilization and\nearly-day convective activity. It should be noted that if a scenario\nwere to occur where the morning storms quickly dissipate, greater \ndestabilization may occur on Tuesday afternoon in advance of \nadditional development, resulting in a greater severe weather risk. \nAs of now, while this scenario is possible, it does not appear to be\nthe most likely solution.\n\nWith so much uncertainty in how storm evolution will play out, the \noverall timing forecast is probably best described diurnally for now,\nroughly 2PM to 9PM. As the cold front moves south into the area, and\nthe atmosphere gets worked over, chances for severe storms should \ndiminish significantly during the mid to late evening.\n\nKey Message 2) Drier and cooler conditions expected through the rest\nof the week.\n\nSurface high pressure will be moving into the area from the north on\nWednesday, behind the cold front mentioned in the section above.\nThis dry and relatively cool air mass will bring max temps down into\nthe 70s on Wednesday. Temps are expected to gradually warm by a few\ndegrees each day from there, with values near normal again (mid to\nupper 80s) over the weekend.",
